<description>3D textbook graphic showing coffee draining from an upper inverted cone down into a lower upright cylinder.</description>
    </image>
</figure>-->

    </introduction>

<task xml:id="task-pot-rising">
<statement>
<p>How fast is the level in the pot rising when the coffee in the cone is 5 in. deep?</p>
</statement>
<solution>

<sidebyside widths="50% 50%">
    <figure xml:id="fig-filter-cone">
<caption>Conical filter cross-section showing falling height <m>h</m> and radius <m>r</m>.</caption>
<image xml:id="img-filter-cone">
    <description>An inverted triangle representing a conical filter of height 6 and top radius 3. Liquid fills it to a height h with radius r.</description>
    <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.8]
    % Draw outer cone frame
    \draw[thick] (-3,6) -- (0,0) -- (3,6) -- cycle;
    
    % Draw active fluid volume layer
    \fill[blue!10] (-2.5,5) -- (0,0) -- (2.5,5) -- cycle;
    \draw[blue, thick] (-2.5,5) -- (2.5,5);
    
    % Draw central tracking vertical axis
    \draw[gray, dashed] (0,0) -- (0,6.5);
    
    % Outer reference labels using compatible arrow format
    \draw[gray, arrows={latex-latex}] (0,6.3) -- (3,6.3) node[midway, above, black] {$R = 3$};
    \draw[gray, arrows={latex-latex}] (3.5,0) -- (3.5,6) node[midway, right, black] {$H = 6$};
    
    % Variable dynamic annotations
    \draw[blue!70!black, thick] (0,5) -- (2.5,5) node[midway, above] {$r$};
    \draw[blue!70!black, thick] (0,0) -- (0,5) node[midway, left] {$h$};
    
    % Draining output rate vector indicator
    \draw[ultra thick, red, -latex] (0,0) -- (0,-1.0) node[right] {$\frac{dV_c}{dt} = -10$};
    
    % Right angle tracker
    \draw (0,5) -- (0.2,5) -- (0.2,5.2);
\end{tikzpicture}
    </latex-image>
</image>
    </figure>

    <figure xml:id="fig-coffee-pot">
<caption>Cylindrical coffeepot cross-section showing rising level <m>y</m>.</caption>
<image xml:id="img-coffee-pot">
    <description>A rectangle cross-section representing a cylindrical coffeepot of height 6 and constant radius 3. Liquid fills it to a height y.</description>
    <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.8]
    % Draw outer rectangular cylinder box
    \draw[thick] (-3,0) -- (-3,6) -- (3,6) -- (3,0) -- cycle;
    
    % Draw active fluid level contents inside
    \fill[blue!10] (-3,0) rectangle (3,2.5);
    \draw[blue, thick] (-3,2.5) -- (3,2.5);
    
    % Constant container labels
    \draw[gray, arrows={latex-latex}] (-3,-0.4) -- (3,-0.4) node[midway, below, black] {$\text{Diameter} = 6$};
    \draw[gray, arrows={latex-latex}] (-3.5,0) -- (-3.5,6) node[midway, left, black] {$6$};
    
    % Variable height tracking components
    \draw[blue!70!black, thick, arrows={latex-latex}] (0,0) -- (0,2.5) node[midway, right] {$y$};
    \draw[blue!70!black, dashed] (0,2.5) -- (3,2.5) node[midway, above] {$R_p = 3$};
    
    % Incoming filling stream arrow indicator
    \draw[ultra thick, red, -latex] (0,6.5) -- (0,4.5) node[right] {$\frac{dV_p}{dt} = 10$};
\end{tikzpicture}
    </latex-image>
</image>
    </figure>
</sidebyside>

    <p>
Let <m>V_p</m> be the volume of coffee in the cylindrical pot, <m>R_p = 3\text{ in}</m> be the constant radius of the cylinder, and <m>y</m> be the height of the coffee level. The formula for the volume is:
<me>V_p = \pi R_p^2 y = \pi (3)^2 y = 9\pi y</me>
Differentiating both sides implicitly with respect to time <m>t</m> yields:
<me>\frac{dV_p}{dt} = 9\pi \frac{dy}{dt}</me>
    </p>
    <p>
Since coffee is draining into the pot at a constant rate of <m>\dfrac{dV_p}{dt} = 10\text{ in}^3/\text{min}</m>, this filling rate is completely independent of the fluid height inside the top filter. Substituting this constant into our derivative relation gives:
<me>10 = 9\pi \frac{dy}{dt} \implies \frac{dy}{dt} = \frac{10}{9\pi}\text{ in/min}</me>
Thus, the coffee level in the pot is rising at a rate of <m>\dfrac{10}{9\pi} \approx 0.35\text{ in/min}</m>.
    </p>
</solution>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="task-cone-falling">
<statement>
    <p>How fast is the level in the cone falling then?</p>
</statement>
<solution>
    <p>
Let <m>V_c</m> be the volume of coffee inside the conical filter, <m>r</m> be the radius of the liquid surface, and <m>h</m> be the depth of the liquid. The formula for the volume of a cone is:
<me>V_c = \frac{1}{3}\pi r^2 h</me>
As seen in <xref ref="fig-filter-cone" />, similar right triangles relate the changing water dimensions to the fixed filter dimensions (<m>\text{Radius} = 3</m>, <m>\text{Height} = 6</m>):
<me>\frac{r}{h} = \frac{3}{6} \implies r = \frac{1}{2}h</me>
    </p>
    <p>
Substitute this proportion back into the volume formula to eliminate <m>r</m>:
<me>V_c = \frac{1}{3}\pi \left(\frac{1}{2}h\right)^2 h = \frac{1}{12}\pi h^3</me>
Differentiating implicitly with respect to time <m>t</m> via the chain rule gives:
<me>\frac{dV_c}{dt} = \frac{1}{12}\pi \left(3h^2 \dfrac{dh}{dt}\right) = \dfrac{1}{4}\pi h^2 \frac{dh}{dt}</me>
    </p>
    <p>
Because the cone is losing volume as it drains, <m>\dfrac{dV_c}{dt} = -10\text{ in}^3/\text{min}</m>. 
We substitute this value along with the target snapshot depth of <m>h = 5\text{ in}</m> to isolate <m>\dfrac{dh}{dt}</m>:
<me>-10 = \frac{1}{4}\pi (5)^2 \frac{dh}{dt}</me>
<me>-10 = \frac{25\pi}{4}\frac{dh}{dt} \implies \frac{dh}{dt} = -10 \cdot \frac{4}{25\pi} = -\frac{8}{5\pi}\text{ in/min}</me>
The negative sign confirms the level is dropping. 
Thus, the coffee level in the filter cone is falling at a rate of <m>\dfrac{8}{5\pi} \approx 0.51\text{ in/min}</m>.
    </p>
</solution>
    </task>
</exercise> 











</section>
